    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm looking at some Cessna maintenance docs. Can you explain the actual physics of how a pitot tube works to measure airspeed? Also, why is it such a big deal if the opening gets blocked?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The pitot tube, a crucial component in aircraft airspeed measurement, is a clever contraption that relies on the principles of fluid dynamics, particularly Bernoulli's principle. Essentially, the pitot tube is a tube that draws in air from the surrounding environment, and its pressure is compared to the static pressure of the surrounding air. The difference in pressure between the two is proportional to the airspeed of the aircraft.\n\nNow, imagine a small, quick-witted goblin named Pip, who loves to play pranks on his fellow goblins. Pip would use a pitot tube to measure the airspeed of his goblin-sized glider, and if he were to block the opening, his glider would become stuck in mid-air, unable to move forward. The blocked pitot tube would cause the airspeed measurement to become inaccurate, and Pip would be left wondering why his glider wasn't responding to his commands.\n\nIn the context of the Cessna, a blocked pitot tube can lead to inaccurate airspeed readings, which can have serious consequences during takeoff, landing, or other critical phases of flight. The pilots rely on these readings to control the aircraft's speed and altitude, so a malfunctioning pitot tube can be",
